## Migrated ASP.NET Core (.NET 8) Code

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class ReportController : Controller
{
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();
        var bytes = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data);
        return File(bytes, "text/csv", "report.csv");
    }

    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Redirect()
    {
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            return RedirectToAction("Index", "Login"); // or Redirect("/login")
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }
}
```

## What Changed and Why

| Old Pattern | Problem | Replacement |
|---|---|---|
| `Response.End()` | Internally called `Thread.Abort` — throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+ | Just `return` from the action; the framework handles response completion |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | The `true` (endResponse) param triggered `Thread.Abort` | Return `Redirect(url)` — an `IActionResult` that sets the 302 and ends the pipeline cleanly |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | No longer thrown; was only needed because `Redirect(url, true)` aborted the thread | Remove entirely — `Redirect()` returns normally, no exception |
| `Response.Write` / `Response.Clear` / `AddHeader` | Low-level response manipulation | Use `File()` result for downloads — handles headers, content type, and streaming |

## Key Pitfalls

1. **Don't write to `Response.Body` then return a result** — pick one. Either return an `IActionResult` (preferred) or write directly to `HttpResponse` and return `new EmptyResult()`.

2. **`Response.Redirect` in ASP.NET Core doesn't throw** — it just sets the 302 header. Code after it **will execute**. Always `return Redirect(...)` to exit the action.

3. **No `ThreadAbortException` exists in .NET 8** — if you port the catch block, it compiles but never triggers. Remove it.

4. **For long-running report generation**, use `HttpContext.RequestAborted`:
   ```csharp
   [HttpPost]
   public async Task<IActionResult> ExportLarge(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
   {
       // ASP.NET Core automatically binds HttpContext.RequestAborted here
       var data = await GenerateReportAsync(cancellationToken);
       return File(data, "text/csv", "report.csv");
   }
   ```

5. **Authentication** — in ASP.NET Core, use the `[Authorize]` attribute instead of manual `IsAuthenticated` checks:
   ```csharp
   [Authorize]
   [HttpPost]
   public IActionResult Export() { ... }
   ```
   Unauthenticated users are automatically redirected to the login page via `AddAuthentication` middleware configuration.
